    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>For more than three thousand years the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> worshipped many gods, among them Ra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, Ptah and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a>, whose cult, carried out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> and the temples, upheld maat, the order of the world, against chaos.</li><li>Its hope of eternal life inspired the pyramids, mummification, richly furnished tombs and the spells of the Book of the Dead, in which the heart of the dead was weighed before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> against the feather of maat.</li><li>Briefly overturned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>’s worship of the sun disc, it endured through Persian, Greek and Roman rule, carried the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, and faded with the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The catacombs dedicated to the jackal god Anubis at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a> held some eight million mummified animals, most of them dogs, offered by pilgrims to the god.</p></aside>
    </section>

