    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 3100 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> is united under the first kings, who rule as the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> on earth</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2560 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Great Pyramid is built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a> as the tomb of the king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khufu">Khufu</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 2350 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>’s oldest religious writings, are carved in the pyramid of Unas</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1350 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> raises the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>, the sun disc, above all other gods and founds a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">30 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; its emperors are portrayed on temple walls as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">391</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapeum-of-alexandria">Serapeum of Alexandria</a> is destroyed after the emperor Theodosius bans pagan sacrifice</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">394</span><span class="rf-text">The last known hieroglyphic inscription is carved at the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 537</span><span class="rf-text">Officials of the emperor Justinian close the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

