    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/narmer"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narmer.jpg" alt="Narmer" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Narmer_palette_(obverse)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The religion of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> grew from the beliefs of the farming communities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley. Graves of the prehistoric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqada">Naqada</a> cultures, from about 4000 BCE, already contain pottery, food, tools and ornaments for the dead, and animals were buried with care, signs of beliefs in an afterlife and in the sacred power of animals that run through the whole history of Egyptian religion.</p><p>Each district of Egypt had its own gods, often in animal form: the falcon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, the jackal Anubis, the cow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>, the crocodile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobek">Sobek</a>, the ram Khnum. When the land was united under a single king around 3100 BCE, kingship itself became sacred. The king was the living Horus, the son of the gods and their representative on earth, and on the ceremonial palette of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narmer">Narmer</a>, one of the first kings, he is shown smiting an enemy, with the falcon of Horus beside him.</p><p>During the Old Kingdom, from about 2686 to 2181 BCE, the cult of the sun god Ra of Heliopolis became the religion of the kings, who from the 4th dynasty called themselves sons of Ra. Their pyramids, culminating in the Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-pyramid-of-giza">Pyramid of Khufu</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, were machines for the king’s resurrection and ascent to the sky, and the kings of the 5th dynasty also built temples to the sun. Priestly centres developed rival accounts of creation: Heliopolis taught that the god Atum arose from the primeval waters on the first mound of earth and created the other gods; Memphis credited its god Ptah, who created by thought and word; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermopolis">Hermopolis</a> spoke of eight primordial deities.</p><p>From around the 24th century BCE the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>, a king murdered by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a> and restored to life by his sister and wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, rose to great importance as lord of the dead. His myth promised resurrection, first to the king and later to everyone, and his cult centre at Abydos became the goal of pilgrims for two thousand years.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="beliefs">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Beliefs &amp; Practice</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/osiris"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osiris.jpg" alt="Osiris" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standing_Osiris.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jeff Dahl, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> worshipped hundreds of gods, the netjeru, who could take human, animal or mixed forms and combine with one another: the sun god Ra merged with the Theban god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> into Amun-Ra, king of the gods. Ra sailed across the sky in his boat by day and through the underworld by night, fighting the serpent Apophis, the embodiment of chaos, before rising again each morning. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a>, the ibis-headed god, was the god of writing and wisdom; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> the goddess of love, music and joy; Ptah the patron of craftsmen; Anubis the guardian of embalming and the cemeteries; Bes and Taweret protected households and childbirth.</p><p>The central idea of Egyptian religion was maat: truth, justice and the right order of the world, established at creation and personified as a goddess with a feather on her head. Maat was constantly threatened by isfet, chaos, and had to be upheld by the king, who offered maat to the gods, and by every person who lived justly. In return the gods sustained the world: the sun rose, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> flooded and the land was fertile.</p><p>The myth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> gave hope beyond death. Osiris, killed and dismembered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a>, was reassembled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, who conceived their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> by him; Horus avenged his father and won the kingship, while Osiris became ruler of the realm of the dead. Every dead person hoped to become an Osiris and to live forever.</p><p>The Egyptians believed that a person had several spiritual elements: the ka, the life force, which needed food and drink; the ba, the personality, shown as a bird with a human head, which could leave the tomb by day; and the akh, the transfigured spirit of the blessed dead. The body had to be preserved by mummification so that these could return to it. The dead faced judgement in the hall of Osiris, where the heart was weighed against the feather of maat; the dead declared their innocence of a long list of sins, and a heart heavy with wrongdoing was devoured by the monster Ammit, while the justified passed to a life of plenty in the Field of Reeds. Magic, heka, was itself a divine power, used in medicine, protection and the spells that guided the dead.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="texts">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Texts &amp; Teachers</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/edfu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edfu.jpg" alt="Edfu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Edfou_Egypte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Patrick.reb, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Egyptian religion had no single sacred book; its texts were written for specific purposes, above all to help the dead. The oldest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a>, are spells for the king’s resurrection and ascent to the stars and the sun, first carved in the burial chambers of the pyramid of Unas, around 2350 BCE. In the Middle Kingdom the Coffin Texts, written inside the coffins of officials and nobles, extended such spells to a wider elite.</p><p>From the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, around 1550 BCE, the dead were buried with the Book of the Dead, or “Spells for Going Forth by Day”, a collection of nearly two hundred spells written on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> and often beautifully illustrated, such as the papyrus of the scribe Ani, of about 1250 BCE, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. Its 125th spell describes the judgement of the dead. The walls of the royal tombs in the Valley of the Kings were covered with books of the underworld, such as the Amduat and the Book of Gates, which map the sun’s night journey.</p><p>Hymns to the gods were inscribed in temples and tombs; the Great Hymn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>, from the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, celebrates the sun as the creator of all life in terms often compared with Psalm 104 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>. Wisdom literature, the instructions of sages to their sons, taught the conduct pleasing to the gods; one of them, the Instruction of Amenemope, closely parallels a passage of the biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-proverbs">Book of Proverbs</a>.</p><p>Egyptian sources usually allude to myths rather than narrate them, and the fullest connected account of the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> comes from the Greek writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, around 100 CE. The temples of the Ptolemaic period, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a>, Dendera and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>, preserve on their walls the most complete records of temple rituals, festivals and myths.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/philae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philae.jpg" alt="Philae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1977_bis_1980_wurde_die_Tempel_auf_der_Insel_Philae_auf_die_h%C3%B6her_gelegene_Insel_Agilka_versetzt._01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Holger Uwe Schmitt, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Egyptian religion developed over three thousand years. In the Middle Kingdom, from about 2055 to 1650 BCE, the hope of eternal life once reserved for kings was extended to all who could afford a proper burial, and pilgrims flocked to Abydos for the festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, from about 1550 to 1069 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> became an empire, and its wealth flowed to the gods. The temple of Amun-Ra at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, enlarged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> after pharaoh, became one of the largest religious complexes ever built, and its priesthood one of the richest institutions in Egypt. Around 1350 BCE the pharaoh Amenhotep IV broke with this tradition: renaming himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, he exalted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>, the visible disc of the sun, above all other gods, closed or neglected their temples, had the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> hacked out of monuments and founded a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a>. After his death, under the young Tutankhamun, the old gods were restored, and later kings erased Akhenaten from the record.</p><p>Egyptian religion survived foreign conquest. Persian kings from 525 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, who visited the oracle of Amun at Siwa and was greeted as the god’s son, and the Ptolemies who ruled after him all presented themselves as pharaohs, and the Ptolemies built great temples in the traditional style at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a>, Dendera, Kom Ombo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>. They also promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapis">Serapis</a>, a god combining Egyptian and Greek features. Under Roman rule from 30 BCE the emperors were depicted on temple walls as pharaohs making offerings to the gods.</p><p>The cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> spread across the Hellenistic and Roman world, with temples from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delos">Delos</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompeii">Pompeii</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> frontier; the Roman novelist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apuleius">Apuleius</a> described initiation into her mysteries in the 2nd century. In Egypt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> spread from the 2nd and 3rd centuries, and by the late 4th century imperial laws banned pagan sacrifice. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serapeum-of-alexandria">Serapeum of Alexandria</a> was destroyed in 391, and the last known hieroglyphic inscription was carved at Philae in 394. The temple of Isis at Philae, on the southern frontier, where Nubian peoples continued to worship, was closed by officials of the emperor Justinian around 537, though worship there may already have dwindled.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="community">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Community &amp; Society</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karnak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karnak.jpg" alt="Karnak" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_de_Louxor_68.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">René Hourdry, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> was in theory the high priest of every god, the one who offered maat and sustained the cosmos, and temple reliefs always show him making the offerings. In practice the daily cult was performed by priests: the hem-netjer, “servants of the god”, led by a high priest, and the wab, “pure ones”, who had to shave their bodies, wash several times a day and wear linen. Many priests served in rotating groups for a month at a time and lived ordinary lives the rest of the year; lector priests read the sacred texts.</p><p>The temple was the house of the god, whose statue lived in a dark sanctuary at its heart. Every morning priests opened the shrine, woke, washed, clothed and fed the god with offerings, which afterwards passed to the priests and temple staff. Ordinary people could not enter the inner temple. They met their gods at festivals, when the statue was carried out in a portable boat, as in the Opet festival at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> travelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>; they also consulted the gods as oracles, whose movements answered questions.</p><p>Temples were great economic institutions, owning land, herds, workshops and granaries, and employing thousands of people. Sacred animals were kept at many of them, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apis">Apis bull</a> of Memphis, buried with royal honours in the Serapeum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a>. In the Late Period pilgrims offered millions of mummified animals, ibises for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a>, cats for Bastet and dogs for Anubis.</p><p>Everyday religion was practised at home and at local shrines, through amulets such as the scarab and the eye of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, prayers to household gods, letters written to dead relatives and offerings to ancestors. The records of Deir el-Medina, the village of the workmen who built the royal tombs, reveal a personal piety in which ordinary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> asked the gods for forgiveness and help.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-simbel.jpg" alt="Abu Simbel" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramsis,_Aswan_Governorate,_Egypt_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">youssef_alam, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ancient Egyptian religion left the most spectacular monuments of the ancient world: the pyramids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>, the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a>, and the painted tombs of the Valley of the Kings, all built for the gods and the afterlife. Its practice of mummification and its belief in a judgement of the dead have fascinated the world ever since.</p><p>Its influence reached far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a> was one of the most popular religions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. Greek writers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> onwards regarded Egypt as the source of ancient wisdom, and the Hermetic writings, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes">Hermes</a> Trismegistus, a Greek form of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a>, shaped the esoteric thought of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. Scholars have compared the Great Hymn to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> with the Psalms, the Instruction of Amenemope with Proverbs, and images of Isis nursing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> with Christian images of the Virgin and Child.</p><p>With the triumph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> the temples were closed and knowledge of the hieroglyphic script was lost. Its decipherment by Jean-François Champollion in 1822, and discoveries such as the tomb of Tutankhamun, found intact by Howard Carter in 1922, gave birth to Egyptology and to waves of popular fascination with Egypt.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>’s worship of the Aten has been debated as one of the first experiments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a>. The Coptic language, the last stage of Egyptian, survives in the liturgy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptic-orthodox-church">Coptic Church</a>, and some modern groups seek to revive the worship of the ancient gods.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ancient Egyptian religion, the faith of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> for more than three thousand years from about 3100 BCE, worshipped Ra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> and hundreds of gods to uphold maat, the order of the world, and promised eternal life to the justified dead. It built the pyramids and the great temples, survived foreign rulers, spread the cult of Isis through the Roman world, and ended with the closure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philae">Philae</a> around 537.</em></p>

